{"name":"Saltbox Blue","hex":"#648fa4","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"saltbox","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A box for keeping salt in.","A similar box formerly used as a percussion instrument in burlesque music."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/saltbox"},{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"}],"entries":[{"title":"Saltbox house","blocks":[{"text":"A saltbox house is a gable-roofed residential structure that is typically two stories in the front and one in the rear, so that the front roof is considerably shorter than the back one. It is a traditional New England-style of home, originally timber-framed, which takes its name from its resemblance to a type of slant-topped lidded wooden box in which salt was once kept."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c1/Thomas_Lee_House%2C_East_Lyme_%28New_London_County%2C_Connecticut%29.jpg/330px-Thomas_Lee_House%2C_East_Lyme_%28New_London_County%2C_Connecticut%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saltbox_house"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8a/Nehemiah_Royce_House%2C_Wallingford%2C_Connecticut.JPG/500px-Nehemiah_Royce_House%2C_Wallingford%2C_Connecticut.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8a/Nehemiah_Royce_House%2C_Wallingford%2C_Connecticut.JPG/1280px-Nehemiah_Royce_House%2C_Wallingford%2C_Connecticut.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8a/Nehemiah_Royce_House%2C_Wallingford%2C_Connecticut.JPG/500px-Nehemiah_Royce_House%2C_Wallingford%2C_Connecticut.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Nehemiah Royce House, Wallingford, Connecticut, c. 1672","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ANehemiah_Royce_House%2C_Wallingford%2C_Connecticut.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3f/Thomas_Hawley_House_Monroe%2C_Connecticut_rear_view.jpg/330px-Thomas_Hawley_House_Monroe%2C_Connecticut_rear_view.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3f/Thomas_Hawley_House_Monroe%2C_Connecticut_rear_view.jpg/960px-Thomas_Hawley_House_Monroe%2C_Connecticut_rear_view.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3f/Thomas_Hawley_House_Monroe%2C_Connecticut_rear_view.